  {
    "id": "Q16",
    "category": "in_scope",
    "question": "Why is the bottom-up BUILD-MAX-HEAP algorithm O(n)?",
    "gold_ids": [
      "8ebfeb1c645b10b3709919603e7d51be_MIT6_006F11_lec04-p13-p18-c3",
      "ebf9f8cb1129d112a39066990693ca6f_B7hVxCmfPtM-p12-p13-c23"
    ],
    "retrieved_ids": [
      "8ebfeb1c645b10b3709919603e7d51be_MIT6_006F11_lec04-p8-p13-c2",
      "ebf9f8cb1129d112a39066990693ca6f_B7hVxCmfPtM-p16-p16-c31",
      "ebf9f8cb1129d112a39066990693ca6f_B7hVxCmfPtM-p12-p12-c22",
      "8ebfeb1c645b10b3709919603e7d51be_MIT6_006F11_lec04-p13-p18-c3",
      "ebf9f8cb1129d112a39066990693ca6f_B7hVxCmfPtM-p9-p10-c17"
    ],
    "confidence": 0.627804696559906,
    "answer": "Based on the lecture materials, the bottom-up `BUILD-MAX-HEAP` algorithm is $O(n)$ for the following reasons:\n\n* **Leaf nodes require no work:** The algorithm starts at $n/2$ because elements from $n/2 + 1$ to $n$ are leaves of the tree and require no work [1, 3]. \n* **Varying work per level:** `Max-Heapify` takes $O(1)$ time for nodes that are one level above the leaves, and in general $O(l)$ time for nodes that are $l$ levels above the leaves [4]. \n* **Fewer nodes at higher levels:** While nodes higher up in the tree (closer to the root) require a logarithmic number of operations, there are fewer and fewer nodes at those higher levels (e.g., there is only one root node, whereas there are $n/4$ nodes at level 1, $n/8$ at level 2, etc.) [3, 4].\n* **Summing the work:** The total amount of work in the loop can be summed as: \n  $$\\frac{n}{4}(1c) + \\frac{n}{8}(2c) + \\frac{n}{16}(3c) + \\dots + 1(\\lg n \\cdot c)$$\n  When simplified, this series is bounded by a constant, resulting in an overall complexity of $O(n)$ [4].",
    "provider": "Gemini (cache)",
